             <p>Remark 3.3 says \(Q(F_k) = x_k\). The condition in Definition 3.1 is \(F(x) \ge p\), so the value whose distribution function equals \(p\) qualifies. In Example 3.6, \(Q(\tfrac35) = 0\), not 1. By Proposition 3.2 with \(F(0) = \tfrac35\) and \(F(1) = \tfrac45\), \(Q(p) = 1\) exactly for \(p\) in \((\tfrac35, \tfrac45]\), as the value \(Q(\tfrac{61}{100}) = 1\) shows.</p>
           </li>
           <li class="misconception">
-            <p class="wrong">“The 99th percentile of 150 values is the 148th smallest, because \(0.99 \times 150 = 148.5\) rounds down.”</p>
-            <p>Proposition 3.4 gives the index \(\lceil pn \rceil = \lceil 148.5 \rceil = 149\). The index is the least integer at least \(pn\), so the ceiling of 148.5 is 149, not the floor, 148. The 149th value is the one whose distribution function reaches 0.99 in the list of 150 equally likely values.</p>
+            <p class="wrong">“The 99th percentile of 150 distinct values is the 148th smallest, because \(0.99 \times 150 = 148.5\) rounds down.”</p>
+            <p>Proposition 3.4 gives, for 150 distinct equally likely values, the index \(\lceil pn \rceil = \lceil 148.5 \rceil = 149\). The index is the least integer at least \(pn\), so the ceiling of 148.5 is 149, not the floor, 148. The 149th value is the one whose distribution function reaches 0.99 in the list of 150 equally likely distinct values.</p>
           </li>
         </ol>
       </section>
