             <span class="problem-title">R3 · What finite checks can and cannot establish</span>
             <p>A colleague says the grid check of Lab 1 proves Proposition 3.2 for this table because every grid point agrees. State precisely what the check establishes, and what proof would be needed for all \(p\).</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>The grid check establishes agreement at the 100 points \(j/100\), \(j = 1, \dots, 100\), for this one table. It does not establish agreement at any other \(p\), and it says nothing about other tables. A proof for all \(p\) is Proposition 3.2 itself: it takes an arbitrary \(p\) in \((0, 1]\), finds the least index with \(F_k \ge p\), and shows that every \(x\) with \(F(x) \ge p\) is at least \(x_{k^*}\). That argument uses only Proposition 2.2 and the definition of infimum, and it covers every table with positive weights.</p>
+              <p>The grid check establishes agreement at the 100 points \(j/100\), \(j = 1, \dots, 100\), for this one table. It does not establish agreement at any other \(p\), and it says nothing about other tables. A proof for all \(p\) is Proposition 3.2 itself: it takes an arbitrary \(p\) in \((0, 1]\), finds the least index with \(F_k \ge p\), and shows that every \(x\) with \(F(x) \ge p\) is at least \(x_{k^*}\). That argument uses only Proposition 2.2 and the definition of infimum, and it covers every table with positive weights and distinct values.</p>
             </details>
           </div>
         </div>
